flowchart LR
%% Discord is the whole human interface here, and worth being precise about why that is safe rather
%% than just convenient. Every webhook is bound to one channel and can only add a message to it: no
%% history, no reactions, no other channel. Two producers that share a channel therefore have to
%% share a webhook, which is why #alerts takes both cluster alerts and reconcile errors. The lines
%% do that work, not words on them.

  subgraph producers["Producers"]
    am["Alertmanager"]
    fluxnotif["Flux notifications"]
    github["GitHub webhook"]
    ci["CI"]
    hermes["Hermes"]
  end

  subgraph channels["Discord channels"]
    alerts["#alerts<br/>cluster alerts and<br/>reconcile errors"]
    security["#security<br/>policy and scan findings"]
    prs["#pull-requests<br/>every change,<br/>and failed checks"]
    general["#general"]
  end

  am --> alerts
  am --> security
  fluxnotif --> alerts
  github --> prs
  ci --> prs
  hermes --> general

  %% Green is where everything lands. Hermes is blue because it is the one producer here that is
  %% not a channel webhook: it holds an app token, minted short-lived by a sidecar, and posts to the
  %% channels it was granted rather than being confined to one.
  classDef ok fill:#17251d,stroke:#4f8f6a,stroke-width:2px,color:#dce9e0
  classDef control fill:#121820,stroke:#71b7ff,stroke-width:2px,color:#e7edf3

  class alerts,security,prs,general ok
  class hermes control